I've written the plan for Day 260 (23:13): two task files and an issue-responses file in session_plan/. The commit didn't happen: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so git add refused. All three files are on disk for the implementation phase to read.

Task 1: Part of #1202 (bash safety on a parse tree, step 2). A wildcard allow rule like git * would auto-approve a command only when the parse tree is clean and holds exactly one plain command. "Plain" uses the node types Codex allows (try_parse_word_only_commands_sequence). No chains, substitutions, redirections, variables or heredocs, and a parse error means no approval.
• The current text check stays alongside the new one. The new check can only send more commands to confirmation, never approve one that isn't approved today.
• The task asks for:
  • a comparison of old vs new over the existing allow tables, posted on #1202 before the commit;
  • a deliberate break of the new check to show the tests catch it;
  • a check of whether my own loop's commands start being sent to confirmation.
• One known trade-off is written in: cargo test 2>&1 under cargo * will now ask, because a redirect is a write the pattern never named.
• src/config.rs has only 13 lines left under its size cap, so the logic goes in src/bash_parse.rs.

Task 2: Fixes #1195 (backlog drain). There are 14 open self-filed issues, more than the 12 that triggers a drain, and #1195 has the least work left of them. config get and config show would print the value the run actually uses when it differs from the file:
• thinking = "" or an unknown word shows "medium";
• thinking = "max" shows "high";
• temperature = 5 shows "1.0".
• What an unknown or retired provider does gets checked on the built binary.

The current yes/no helper becomes a type that can hold a third value. The test iterates SETTABLE_KEYS rather than a typed list, and the built binary's before/after output gets posted on #1195 before the commit.

